{"name":"Rice Curry","hex":"#b2854a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Cereal plants, Oryza sativa of the grass family whose seeds are used as food.","A specific variety of this plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To squeeze through a ricer; to mash or make into rice-sized pieces (especially potatoes).","To harvest wild rice (Zizania spp.)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rice"},{"term":"curry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of a family of dishes originating from Indian cuisine, flavored by a spiced sauce.","A spiced sauce or relish, especially one flavored with curry powder."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cook or season with curry powder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/curry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Curry rice","blocks":[{"text":"Curry rice may refer any rice dish prepared with curry:"},{"items":["Rice and curry, a popular dish in Sri Lanka","a type of Japanese curry","Nasi kari, an Indian-Indonesian rice dish","Dal bhat, a lentil and rice dish in South Asia"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curry_rice"},{"title":"Rice","blocks":[{"text":"Rice is a cereal grain and, in its domesticated form, is the staple food of over half of the world's population, particularly in Africa and Asia. Rice is the seed of the grass species Oryza sativa —or, less commonly, Oryza glaberrima. Asian rice was domesticated in China some 13,500 to 8,200 years ago; African rice was domesticated independently in West Africa about 3,000 years ago. Rice has become commonplace in many cultures worldwide; in 2023, 800 million tons were produced, placing it third after sugarcane and maize. Only some 8% of rice is traded internationally. China, India, and Indonesia are the largest consumers of rice. A substantial amount of the rice produced in developing nations is lost after harvest through factors such as poor transport and storage. Rice yields can be reduced by pests including insects, rodents, and birds, as well as by weeds, and by diseases such as rice blast. Traditional rice polycultures such as rice-duck farming, and modern integrated pest management seek to control damage from pests in a sustainable way."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/20201102.Hengnan.Hybrid_rice_Sanyou-1.6.jpg/330px-20201102.Hengnan.Hybrid_rice_Sanyou-1.6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice"},{"title":"Curry","blocks":[{"text":"Curry is a dish with a spicy sauce, initially in Indian cuisine, then modified by interchange with the Portuguese, followed by the British, and eventually thoroughly internationalised. Many curries are found in the cuisines of countries in Southeast Asia and East Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Taj_Mahal_-_Lamb_Curry_Madras.jpg/330px-Taj_Mahal_-_Lamb_Curry_Madras.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curry"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Rice_grains_%28IRRI%29.jpg/500px-Rice_grains_%28IRRI%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Rice_grains_%28IRRI%29.jpg/1280px-Rice_grains_%28IRRI%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Rice_grains_%28IRRI%29.jpg/500px-Rice_grains_%28IRRI%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rice grains of different varieties at the International Rice Research Institute (IRRI)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARice_grains_(IRRI).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Oryza_sativa_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-232.jpg